public class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends a renewal-reminder email to the owner of each opportunity in the list.
     * Each email is sent individually so that one failure does not prevent the rest.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds  Up to 200 Opportunity Ids.
     * @return The number of emails that were sent successfully.
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Single bulk SOQL – well within the 100-query / 50k-row limits.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        Integer sentCount = 0;

        for (Id oppId : opportunityIds) {
            Opportunity opp = oppMap.get(oppId);

            // Skip records that were not returned (deleted, no access, etc.)
            if (opp == null) {
                continue;
            }

            // Skip if there is no owner to notify
            if (opp.OwnerId == null) {
                continue;
            }

            try {
                Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
                email.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
                email.setSaveAsActivity(false);
                email.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + (opp.Name != null ? opp.Name : 'Unnamed Opportunity'));

                String closeDateText = (opp.CloseDate != null)
                    ? opp.CloseDate.format()
                    : 'not yet set';

                email.setPlainTextBody(
                    'Dear ' + getOwnerName(opp.OwnerId) + ',\n\n' +
                    'The following opportunity has a renewal / close date approaching:\n\n' +
                    '  Opportunity: ' + (opp.Name != null ? opp.Name : 'N/A') + '\n' +
                    '  Close Date:  ' + closeDateText + '\n\n' +
                    'Please follow up with the customer to ensure the renewal is on track.\n\n' +
                    'Regards,\nRenewal Reminder Service'
                );

                Messaging.sendEmail(new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage[] { email });
                sentCount++;

            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Isolate the failure so remaining emails are still attempted.
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'RenewalReminderService: failed to send email for Opportunity '
                    + oppId + ' – ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }

    /**
     * Lightweight helper to fetch the owner's name for the greeting.
     * Falls back to a generic label if the lookup fails.
     */
    private static String getOwnerName(Id ownerId) {
        try {
            List<User> users = [SELECT Name FROM User WHERE Id = :ownerId LIMIT 1];
            if (!users.isEmpty()) {
                return users[0].Name;
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Non-critical – just use a fallback
        }
        return 'there';
    }
}
```

### Note on the extra SOQL in `getOwnerName`

The helper adds one SOQL per iteration (up to 200), which could push you close to the 100-query limit if combined with other queries in the same transaction. If that's a concern, replace it with a single batch query up front:

```apex
// Replace the per-iteration getOwnerName call with a pre-built map:
Map<Id, String> ownerNameMap = new Map<Id, String>(
    [SELECT Id, Name FROM User WHERE Id IN :oppMap.values()
     .stream().filter(o -> o.OwnerId != null).map(o -> o.OwnerId)
     .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.toSet())]
);
```

Or more idiomatically in Apex (no streams):

```apex
Set<Id> ownerIds = new Set<Id>();
for (Opportunity o : oppMap.values()) {
    if (o.OwnerId != null) ownerIds.add(o.OwnerId);
}
Map<Id, String> ownerNameMap = new Map<Id, String>(
    [SELECT Id, Name FROM User WHERE Id IN :ownerIds]
);
```

Then use `ownerNameMap.get(opp.OwnerId)` in the body. That keeps the total SOQL count at **2** regardless of how many opportunities are passed in.

## Why I changed your approach

Loading **all 2.3 million contacts** into a single `Map` will blow past the synchronous heap limit (6 MB). Even with only three fields, the per-record SObject + Map-entry overhead means you'd need well over 1 GB of heap. It would also be a multi-second SOQL for what is, in practice, a very small subset of those records.

Instead, I query **only the contacts whose `AccountId` is in the set of changed accounts**. In your MDM scenario (batches of 200), that's a handful of thousands of records at most — comfortably within limits and a fraction of a second.

Everything else stays the same: one SOQL, no queries in loops, bulk-safe.

